            "paragraphs": [
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "Emissions per person use no population floor. The top of that list is dominated by small petrostates, which is the finding rather than a distortion: Qatar emits 35.6 tonnes per person against a world average of 4.7.",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontWeight": "bold",
                      "fontSize": "9pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": ""
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "The scatter is clipped at 6,000 Mt, which excludes China at 11,472. Its position is not in doubt and including it compresses every other country into the left edge.",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontWeight": "bold",
                      "fontSize": "9pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": ""
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "Page 1 showed coal falling from 100% of emissions to 40%. This page shows the other half of that: the share fell because oil and gas grew faster, not because coal shrank. Coal emissions in 2021 were the highest ever recorded.",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontWeight": "bold",
                      "fontSize": "9pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
